.TH SDL_GetEventDescription 3 "SDL 3.5.0" "Simple Directmedia Layer" "SDL3 FUNCTIONS"
.SH NAME
SDL_GetEventDescription \- Generate an English description of an event.
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
.B #include <SDL3/SDL_events.h>
.PP
.BI "int SDL_GetEventDescription(const SDL_Event *event, char *buf, int buflen);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
This will fill
.BR buf
with a null-terminated string that might look something like this:

.IP
.EX
SDL_EVENT_MOUSE_MOTION (timestamp=1140256324 windowid=2 which=0 state=0 x=492.99 y=139.09 xrel=52 yrel=6)
.EE
.PP

The exact format of the string is not guaranteed; it is intended for logging purposes, to be read by a human, and not parsed by a computer\[char46]
.PP
The returned value follows the same rules as
.BR SDL_snprintf ():
.BR buf
will always be NULL-terminated (unless
.BR buflen
is zero), and will be truncated if
.BR buflen
is too small\[char46] The return code is the number of bytes needed for the complete string, not counting the NULL-terminator, whether the string was truncated or not\[char46] Unlike
.BR SDL_snprintf (),
though, this function never returns
-1\[char46]

.SH FUNCTION PARAMETERS
.TP
.I event
an event to describe\[char46] May be NULL\[char46]
.TP
.I buf
the buffer to fill with the description string\[char46] May be NULL\[char46]
.TP
.I buflen
the maximum bytes that can be written to
.BR buf \[char46]

.SH RETURN VALUE
Returns number of bytes needed for the full string, not counting the null-terminator byte\[char46]

.SH THREAD SAFETY
It is safe to call this function from any thread\[char46]

.SH AVAILABILITY
This function is available since SDL 3\[char46]4\[char46]0\[char46]

